WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.052 --> 00:00:11.260
WILLIAM DENNIS: Hi, everyone.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.220
I'm William Dennis, a Product
Manager on Google Cloud working

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.570
on GKE Autopilot.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.070
I'll be joined later
by my colleague

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.860
Gary, who's going to take
you through a few demos.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.920
I'm excited to talk to you
today about GKE's new Autopilot

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.380
mode of operation to help
streamline your application

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.020
delivery and management.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.257
Before getting into the
details of GKE Autopilot,

00:00:30.257 --> 00:00:32.340
I'd like to quickly go
over some different options

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.110
you have today for
deploying your applications.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:39.280
You can run them the traditional
way on VMs directly using GCE,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.680
or build them for
serverless with Cloud Run.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.580
And sitting in the middle
is Kubernetes with GKE.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.470
As you move up the
layers of abstraction,

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.530
from raw VMs where you manage
everything, through Kubernetes,

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.900
which offers container
orchestration and management,

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.020
to serverless, where you just
provide your container to run,

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.680
your operations get easier as
there's less for you to manage.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.630
But your deployment
choices and flexibility

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.130
is a little bit
reduced as you go up

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:04.959
the stack at the same time.

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:06.340
That's essentially
the trade-off.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.740
You're trading the flexibility
to deploy things however

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.680
you like with the need to manage
all that custom configuration.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.570
So what makes Kubernetes
such a popular choice?

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:19.370
The main reasons I
hear are that you

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.620
have some requirements that
are not necessarily solved

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.357
by a higher order product.

00:01:23.357 --> 00:01:25.190
While it's great if you
can adapt everything

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.680
to your serverless-- and
frankly speaking, if you can,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.330
I would recommend it--

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.400
a lot of the time, the
reality is there's just

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.240
stuff you don't want to change.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.050
Maybe you bring over a bunch
of existing applications.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:39.200
Maybe you need to run a fairly
complex deployment with state--

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.960
that needs an attached
persistent disk.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.398
And sure, in those cases,
people will tell you, hey,

00:01:44.398 --> 00:01:46.190
you need to get rid of
that persistent disk

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:47.690
and go use a managed products.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.840
And well, they might be
right some of the time.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.990
But practically speaking,
it's not always an option.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.150
Maybe the state is
for a custom database

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.760
that you're the one
responsible for managing.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.130
Maybe there is no managed
solution available,

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.850
or maybe you just don't
want to change things.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.358
Kubernetes is a very practical
deployment environment

00:02:06.358 --> 00:02:07.900
which understands
that you have a lot

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.120
of different applications,
some legacy, some new ones,

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.850
and they have
various requirements

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.480
that can be pretty complex.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.350
So it is in turn a fairly
powerful system for handling

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.180
these requirements.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.590
What about compared to VMs?

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.270
Well, if you run
stuff in VMs you

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.968
have to manage all these VMs,
which can be quite a burden.

00:02:24.968 --> 00:02:27.010
You need to worry about
where your application is

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.300
going on which VM.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.460
You have to remember the
health of those VMs, and so on.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.980
Kubernetes sits right in
the middle of this spectrum.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.720
It's not too low level and
it's not too high level,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.070
and that makes it a very
attractive deployment

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.410
platform for a lot of users.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.820
Add to this a few other
properties, like the fact

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.353
it's based on
open-source technology

00:02:46.353 --> 00:02:48.020
so you can move your
deployments around,

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.540
and it becomes very
attractive indeed.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.528
Kubernetes can run
pretty much anywhere.

00:02:52.528 --> 00:02:54.320
So you might be running
Kubernetes on-prem,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.862
you might be running
in other clouds.

00:02:55.862 --> 00:02:57.830
Using Kubernetes in
all these environments

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.540
makes your deployments
standardized and portable.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.070
So what are the
actual components

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:06.130
in Kubernetes that offer
this flexibility that

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:07.660
makes it so attractive?

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.310
Kubernetes ships
with many components

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.500
out of the box,
including Deployment,

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.020
for stateless web
applications, StatefulSet,

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:15.820
for custom databases,
and anything else

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.680
that needs a persistent disk.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.320
For batch jobs,
there's the Job object.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.520
If you need to run an
agent on every node,

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.120
such as to capture logs,
you have the DaemonSet.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.130
Added to this are some
scheduling constructs,

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.620
like zonal affinity,
and pod topology

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.230
spread patterns that allow
you to design your workload

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.560
around failure domains, as well
as priority and preemption that

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.080
give you fine-grained access
to the scheduling priority

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.230
to enable rapid scaling of
high-priority workloads.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.690
At a technical level,
all these constructs

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.700
are represented in
Kubernetes objects

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:47.710
through the Kubernetes API.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.710
You can describe a whole bunch
of things, even at this level,

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.680
at the Kubernetes API level,
what zones you want to run in,

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.777
or how you want to spread
your pods for fault tolerance.

00:03:56.777 --> 00:03:59.110
Say if you want to co-locate
two different apps together

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.620
on a node so that each machine
is running the frontend

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.120
and backend application, while
at the same time being spread

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.010
out over a zone,
you can do that.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:09.190
Now Kubernetes does have a
bit of a reputation for having

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:10.477
a steep learning curve.

00:04:10.477 --> 00:04:12.310
After all, there's a
lot of different things

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.120
here, many of which
I just talked about.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.200
And that reputation may
be somewhat deserved.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.329
But let me make a
couple of claims.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:22.300
Firstly, if you just need
to deploy a simple web

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.860
application, then
as my colleague

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.560
Gary is going to demonstrate,
it's not that hard at all.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.440
You only need to learn
to API constructs--

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.138
a deployment, which
houses your code,

00:04:31.138 --> 00:04:33.430
and a service, which is how
you expose your application

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.140
to the internet-- that's it.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.930
So don't feel like you have to
learn everything all at once,

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:42.640
but know that it's there if
and likely when you need it.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.490
And secondly, as a
professional, you're

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.470
not necessarily always
looking for the simplest

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.060
tools out there.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.990
Simple is good,
don't get me wrong.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.990
But what you really
need are powerful tools.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.660
You want to make sure that the
deployment tools you're using

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.960
can actually deliver all the
things that you need to do.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.730
And Kubernetes is really
good at doing that,

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:03.420
so I'd say it's worth learning.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.823
Now one of the things that
makes Kubernetes a little more

00:05:05.823 --> 00:05:07.740
challenging, though, is
that you traditionally

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.430
need to learn two systems.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:13.190
You need to learn the
Kubernetes architecture-- that

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.440
is, the open-source
API components,

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.570
above the line on
the screen here.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.820
And you also need to learn
the platform API, like GKE's.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.970
At the GKE layer, you create
the cluster and provision

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:28.610
it with the compute capacity
your workloads need, or set up

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.620
autoscalers to do that for you.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:33.680
And once created, you have
a shared responsibility

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.750
for those compute
resources, and may

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.600
need to debug node
issues from time to time.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.585
This is just our
Kubernetes works,

00:05:40.585 --> 00:05:42.710
and the overhead you incur
for getting the benefits

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.680
and power of Kubernetes.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.230
At least, that's
how it used to be.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:51.230
This is why we created
Autopilot for GKE.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:55.370
Autopilot joins what we now call
the standard mode of operation,

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.510
giving you two ways to use GKE.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:01.030
With Autopilot, most
of the platform details

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:03.730
are handled for
you automatically.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.260
It's still the same GKE
that you know and love.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:09.400
We just now have a new
way for you to operate it.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.460
This is the Cluster Creation
page for GKE Autopilot.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.140
As you can see,
it's pretty simple.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.990
In fact, these are
all the settings

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:18.850
you need to get
yourself a functioning,

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:21.160
production-grade cluster.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:23.140
You just need to give
your cluster a name,

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.980
pick the region, and
configure network.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.430
So hopefully coming up
with a name isn't so hard.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.950
As for the region, you may
have a favorite already.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.510
Otherwise, generally
you would just

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.950
pick the one closest
to your users.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.660
For networking, if you
have public networking

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:40.710
you don't need to
do anything more.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.300
For private networks,
you just need

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.400
to specify your subnets
and ranges like normal,

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:46.930
and that's it.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:48.160
That's really it.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.410
Sure, there's a couple of
additional security features

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.630
that you can enable or
disable below the fold,

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:54.880
or you can just accept
our secure defaults

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.700
and change it later
if you need to.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.330
Importantly, there is no node
or autoscaling setup needed.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.400
Just go ahead and click
the Create button,

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.170
get your
production-grade cluster.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.960
Going back to the API view of
the world, what does this mean?

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.270
Well, basically we shrank the
whole GKE API service down

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:19.080
to a single core,
the create core.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.665
As for the Kubernetes
API, which is

00:07:21.665 --> 00:07:23.040
how you represent
your workloads,

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.900
well, that's still there.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.120
And this is really the point.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.970
Our goal with Autopilot is to
support most of those workloads

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:31.740
that GKE standard does today.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:35.670
In particular, we still support
all those stateful workloads

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.320
that need a persistent disk.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.590
Our goal here is not to change
the Kubernetes API, just

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.310
the platform it runs on.

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:44.680
So how does this work?

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:47.460
How can we still offer most of
the Kubernetes features of GKE

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.430
standard, while reducing
the platform API to just the

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:51.720
create function?

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.900
Well, with Autopilot, you
describe your workloads

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.075
in Kubernetes terms,
as you always have,

00:07:57.075 --> 00:08:00.630
like your container image
and the resources it needs.

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.810
Once scheduled on a GKE cluster
running in Autopilot mode,

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.090
we'll automatically figure
out the node resources

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.070
needed to run those
workloads and provision

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:10.310
enough capacity for you.

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:13.240
For example, if your container
requests two CPU cores

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:15.550
and 6 gigabytes of
memory, we'll provision

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:18.887
a node big enough to handle it.

00:08:18.887 --> 00:08:20.470
We apply the best
practices that we've

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.420
learned from six
years of running GKE,

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:26.470
giving you a production-grade
cluster without the hassle.

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:29.110
So that's handy.

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.890
But what about after
the cluster is created?

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.059
This is actually where the real
benefit of Autopilot comes in.

00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:37.010
With a traditional
Kubernetes platform,

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:39.320
including GKE's standard
mode of operation,

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:42.080
there is a shared
responsibility over the nodes.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:45.260
While GKE standard handles
the lifecycle of those nodes,

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:48.560
for example, the creation,
updating, deleting,

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.840
and offers several
features to help automate,

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:54.090
like auto-repair-- at
the end of the day,

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.720
those nodes are still VMs
that you need to manage.

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.480
You have admin access to
them and can potentially

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:03.240
modify them to behave in ways
that GKE might not expect.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.570
So for Autopilot, we
looked at this and asked,

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.430
do you come to GKE
for the ability

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.170
to be the root user
on those nodes?

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:12.780
Or are you primarily looking
to run your own applications

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:14.130
on this platform?

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:17.520
We believe for most, the goal
is to run your own business

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:18.550
applications.

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:20.880
So that's what we offer here.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.730
In this mode you're responsible
for describing your workloads

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.450
and running them.

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:28.120
If your workload crashes
due to a bug in your code,

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:29.920
I hate to say it, it's
still your problem.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:32.140
But most other components
related to the nodes

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:35.560
are now Google's responsibility,
along with the cluster itself.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.350
This is a big deal.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:41.550
And so with this, we
introduced a pod-level SLA

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:42.900
for the first time on GKE.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:44.220
It's three 9s.

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.220
This means you now have a
cluster that's easier to set up

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:48.790
and easier to manage.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:51.090
So as a developer,
you're more productive,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.250
and in production,
you spend less time

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.290
on the minutiae of
managing the thing,

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.780
and more time running
your application, which

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:02.220
is presumably what you're
ultimately here to do.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.470
The third major difference
with this mode of operation

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:07.590
is how resources are built.
Since you describe everything

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:10.290
at a pod level, we bill
you at a pod level as well.

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.270
It's kind of intuitive
and it brings

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.710
with it some nice benefits.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:16.800
One benefit is you don't have
to be a so-called Kubernetes

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:20.400
binpacking expert, because you
no longer care if your node has

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.460
32 pods or 0, or whether
your node's CPU has

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.350
been fully allocated or not.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:28.320
Because all of that
is now our problem.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:31.840
Our system is responsible
for packing pods onto nodes.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:35.160
And if there's any wasted
spare capacity, that's on us.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:37.620
You don't pay for it.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:40.680
So it's a really nice
billing model as well.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.830
And with this comes
another nice attribute.

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:44.660
If you're running
a multi-team setup,

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:47.840
you can now figure out how
much each namespace costs just

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:50.610
by adding up all the pod
requests in that namespace.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.920
And we even have tools
to help you with that.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.970
What's more is that Autopilot
comes with a strong security

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:57.530
posture right out of the box.

00:10:57.530 --> 00:10:59.750
We implement the GKE
hardening guidelines,

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:02.000
and since the nodes are
more locked down in order

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.700
for us to provide
management and that SLA,

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.980
you have the benefit of
the reduced node surface

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:08.240
area as well.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.030
You won't need to worry
about unpatched nodes either,

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:14.310
since we will keep everything
up to date automatically.

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:16.430
And if automatic
updates worry you,

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:18.152
fear not, as we
have several tools

00:11:18.152 --> 00:11:20.360
to manage that as well,
including maintenance windows

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:22.953
and pod disruption budgets.

00:11:22.953 --> 00:11:25.620
So coming back to the Kubernetes
workload constructs I presented

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:27.930
earlier, and one
of the main reasons

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:31.140
why people use Kubernetes in the
first place, let's have a look.

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.150
Do they work on Autopilot?

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:37.530
The answer is, for the most
part, yes, and in the same way.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.050
Deployments, stateful
set, jobs, they

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:41.940
all work exactly the same way.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:45.150
Yes, even that complex
stateful custom database

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:48.660
will just work on Autopilot with
no additional platform-specific

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:49.920
configuration needed.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.080
You can move it right on over.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.390
Let me repeat this,
because it's so important.

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:56.130
We understand that
a lot of users

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:57.870
run stateful workloads
in their clusters.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.900
So when we created Autopilot, we
didn't take any shortcuts here.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:02.820
Everything just works
for stateful set

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.340
as you would expect from GKE.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.530
Now since pods
running on Autopilot

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:10.660
don't have admin privileges,
it does alter some use cases,

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:13.180
especially for DaemonSets.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:16.510
But this is mostly in the domain
of administrative functions.

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:20.320
The exact type of functions, we
hope, won't be needed anymore.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.020
And we've engaged
several key partners

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:23.940
to deliver their
solutions on Autopilot

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.610
for logging, monitoring,
and security in the same way

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:28.740
they work on GKE
standard, covering

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:31.562
many of the popular
DaemonSets that we see.

00:12:31.562 --> 00:12:33.020
There's a couple
of other features,

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:35.480
like GPUs, which GKE
standard offers that's

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.310
currently lacking in Autopilot.

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:40.010
For the full list, see our
comprehensive Autopilot

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:41.270
overview documentation.

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:44.820
I'll put a link in
the description below.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:47.210
So we launched Autopilot
about half a year ago,

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:49.430
and companies like
Ubie are already seeing

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:51.170
the benefit of this approach.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:53.270
Ubie, if you haven't
heard of them yet,

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:55.580
it's an innovative
medical technology startup

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:56.450
based in Japan.

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:58.460
They found that
with Autopilot they

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.397
were able to focus
more on doing the thing

00:13:00.397 --> 00:13:01.980
that they created
their company to do,

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.670
which is to build better
health care solutions, and less

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:08.270
on managing the cluster
and all the infrastructure.

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:09.980
You can read all
about this case study

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:11.930
on our website, which
I highly recommend.

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:14.840
Again, I'll put a link
in the description below.

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:17.090
And with that, I want to
hand it over to my colleague,

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:19.640
Gary, who's going to run
through a practical demo using

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:20.660
Autopilot.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.010
Over to you, Gary.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:24.400
GARI SINGH: Thanks, William.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:26.400
In this demo, we'll try
to deploy a standard web

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.010
application with a Redis
backend on Autopilot.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:31.200
The application has
three services--

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:35.970
a Redis leader with a single
replica, a Redis follower,

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:40.230
which actually has three
replicas, and a Redis

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.470
and a frontend web
application, which has three

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:45.700
replicas as well.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:47.922
Now let's deploy
this application.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:01.480
All right.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:03.105
Now let's check the
status of our pods.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:13.710
Now notice that we have one
in the Ready state and several

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:14.920
in the Pending state.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.850
That's because we actually don't
have enough compute resource

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:19.170
provisioned in the cluster.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:22.110
But not to worry, Autopilot
will handle this for us.

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:28.210
Behind the scenes,
Autopilot is actually

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:29.980
provisioning additional
compute for us.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:30.730
Let's take a look.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:39.980
You can see that here
we've added one new node,

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.280
and we have two new nodes in the
provisioning status right now.

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:47.600
If we take another
look at our pods,

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:50.090
we'll see that we actually
have a couple more

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:52.130
in the ContainerCreating stage.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:54.135
We'll wait for a little
bit, but soon we'll

00:14:54.135 --> 00:14:55.260
see all of our application.

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:57.135
So all of our pods will
actually be deployed.

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:04.820
All right.

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:06.710
Let's check our pod
status one more time.

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:11.870
All right.

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:15.350
All of our pods are Running.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:18.890
We'll also notice we now have
five nodes up and running

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:20.375
as well to meet the demands.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:27.430
Now let's actually test
out this application.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:36.790
We need to get the address
of the frontend application.

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:37.290
All right.

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:41.430
We can see that the external
IP address here is that.

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:43.755
We'll copy that, paste
it into our browser.

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:47.250
All right.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:49.740
We have the Guestbook
application.

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:51.165
We'll type in a
few messages here.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:04.910
All right we'll close this out.

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:08.380
But if all is working, we should
see these messages back again

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:11.120
coming from the cache.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:11.690
All right.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:14.360
Our application is up
and running, all as well.

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:20.160
Now in this demo, we showed
the ability of Autopilot

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:22.440
to automatically and
dynamically provision compute

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:23.880
as needed by our workload.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:26.193
We didn't get into autoscaling,
but William's going

00:16:26.193 --> 00:16:27.360
to tell us a bit about that.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:28.985
And then I'll be back
with another demo

00:16:28.985 --> 00:16:32.090
to show you that as well.

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:33.400
WILLIAM DENNIS: Thanks, Gary.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:36.340
So as Gary showed, Autopilot
will automatically provision

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:38.890
resources to schedule
your pods under the hood,

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:42.460
based on the resources
those pods request.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:45.920
But what about scaling
the workloads themselves?

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:50.650
For this, GKE offers to
pod-level scaling options.

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.320
The vertical pod autoscaler
can increase and decrease

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:55.720
the resources used by the pod.

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:57.940
It does this by
monitoring the resource

00:16:57.940 --> 00:16:59.200
utilization of the pod.

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:00.850
And it can adjust
the pod requests,

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:02.920
either increasing it
to improve performance

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:05.109
when it notices more
CPU might be needed,

00:17:05.109 --> 00:17:07.839
or decreasing it, when
it sees that the CPU is

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:09.490
being underutilized.

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:11.602
And when you Autopilot,
that's all you need to do.

00:17:11.602 --> 00:17:13.060
Autopilot will
ensure that you have

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:15.160
the right node resources
available to run

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:16.240
those resized pods.

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:20.319
And of course, you only pay for
the precise resource requests

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:22.160
of your pod.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:24.680
To enable in GKE
Autopilot, create

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:27.200
a Vertical Pod Autoscaling
object that references

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:28.260
your deployment.

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:30.200
You can optionally
exclude certain containers

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:32.000
in the pod from the VPA.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:33.590
I'll put a link to
the documentation

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:36.000
in the description below.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.620
The other workload
scaling option

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:40.320
is the Horizontal Pod
Autoscaler, or HPA.

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:42.413
The Horizontal
Pod Autoscaler can

00:17:42.413 --> 00:17:44.580
change the number of pod
replicas in your deployment

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:46.820
based on demand.

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:48.380
With the Horizontal
Pod Autoscaler,

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:50.960
you set a metric to
observe, and a target,

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:53.630
and the HPA will attempt
to create more pod replicas

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:55.470
to keep that targeting range.

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:58.670
For example, if you want
to have 1 pod for every 100

00:17:58.670 --> 00:18:00.860
requests per second that
hit your load balancer,

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:02.630
it could do that.

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:05.990
With GKE Autopilot, you can
just set and forget this HPA

00:18:05.990 --> 00:18:07.940
and let it handle the
usage spikes for you,

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:11.240
as Autopilot will provision
and de-provision automatically

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:13.940
the compute resources
needed to handle those pods.

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:18.170
And of course, as always, you
only pay for running pods.

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:20.900
To enable, create a
Horizontal Pod Autoscaling

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:24.710
object that references the
deployment to be scaled,

00:18:24.710 --> 00:18:27.500
specifying the minimum and
maximum replicate counts,

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:30.800
the metric to be observed,
and your desired target.

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:32.750
Horizontal Pod Autoscaling
can be configured

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:35.360
to use CPU, memory,
or custom metrics,

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:38.810
like requests per second,
or pub-sub queue depth.

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:40.790
Now I'd like to hand
it back to Gary, who's

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:43.220
going to show you a really
cool demo of using Autopilot

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:47.360
with a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler
to handle a pub-sub queue.

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:48.623
Back over to you, Gary.

00:18:48.623 --> 00:18:49.790
GARI SINGH: Thanks, William.

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:52.400
In this demo, we're going
to show how to auto scale

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:54.170
a pub-sub workload
based on metrics

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:56.600
provided by Google
Cloud Monitoring.

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:59.780
As you can see, we've already
created a topic named echo,

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:02.540
and a corresponding
subscription named echo-read.

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:05.660
The goal for this demo is to
scale the number of subscribers

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:07.130
for this topic
based on the number

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:10.340
of unacknowledged messages
remaining in the subscription.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.830
As you can see on the
screen, pub-sub conveniently

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:15.140
provides this metric
for us, and later we'll

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:18.200
actually show how to
configure the Horizontal Pod

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:20.450
Autoscaler to use it.

00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:24.410
Now this is all made possible
by using the custom metrics

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:27.500
adapter, which will provide
cloud-monitoring metrics

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:28.590
for our cluster.

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:30.590
Now while William was
talking I actually

00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:34.040
took the liberty of
deploying this adapter,

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:38.060
so let's take a quick look
just to make sure everything's

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:40.440
running.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:40.940
All right.

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:44.360
So now we see we have our custom
metrics adapter up and running.

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:45.950
Now we can actually
check to make sure

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:50.510
that the metric that we want
is available by querying

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:52.340
the Kubernetes
external metrics API.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:57.260
And here in this box,
you'll see that we

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:00.410
have our subscription number
undelivered messages metric.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:04.020
And we actually see that we have
it for our actual subscription.

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:07.340
So we'll be set up to use
this in the HPA later on.

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:13.400
But before we can
deploy our application,

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:15.440
we'll need to
configure an identity

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:19.070
the app can use to authenticate
with Cloud pub-sub.

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:22.440
Autopilot workloads
use workload identity,

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:24.620
which is the recommended
way to access Google Cloud

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:26.340
Services from applications.

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:28.670
It provides improved
security and manageability

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:32.270
over using things like secrets
or mounting token files, which

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:34.400
are insecure.

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:37.700
First, let's create a dedicated
namespace for our application.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:40.640
Great.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:42.770
We'll use the namespace pubsub.

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:44.960
Next, we'll need
to create a service

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:51.890
account that we'll actually
use in our deployment metadata.

00:20:51.890 --> 00:20:55.110
We'll call it
pubsub-service-account.

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:58.235
We also need to create a
Google Cloud service account.

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:07.270
And we'll need to
give it permission

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:09.520
to actually access pubsub.

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:11.980
Here you see we're giving
it the pubsub.editor role.

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:19.170
Next, we'll actually bind the
Kubernetes service account

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:20.550
with the Google service account.

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:29.930
And finally, we'll annotate
the Kubernetes service account

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:31.430
so that it knows
about this binding.

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:39.570
Now let's take a look at our
actual application itself.

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:45.640
The actual application
itself is a simple app

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:49.900
which uses a Python SDK to
actually just pull messages

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:52.100
from the subscription.

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:55.310
Now our deployment for this
application is pretty simple.

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:56.860
We have a standard container.

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:59.920
We're going to name it pubsub.

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:02.980
And as you can see
here, we've actually

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:04.690
chosen the serviceAccount
under which

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:09.340
this particular deployment
will actually run.

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:12.760
Now along with that, we
also have a Horizontal Pod

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:14.620
Autoscaler resource.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:17.920
And as you can see here, we
showed this metric earlier.

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:20.590
We're using the external metric.

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:24.580
We're using the
undelivered messages.

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:28.240
We want this to be for our
echo-read subscription.

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:31.240
And our goal here will be
that we never want to--

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:32.930
no matter how many
subscribers we have,

00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:36.040
we want the average value of
the backlog to actually be 5.

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:42.310
All right.

00:22:42.310 --> 00:22:44.890
So let's actually
deploy our application.

00:22:47.950 --> 00:22:50.463
First, we'll just set
the default namespace

00:22:50.463 --> 00:22:51.130
for our context.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:57.810
Then we'll actually
deploy our pod.

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:09.240
We'll check to make
sure our pod is running.

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:13.020
All right.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:15.930
We've successfully
deployed that.

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:19.320
Next, we'll actually
deploy the HPA resource.

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:33.450
Let's take a quick look.

00:23:39.075 --> 00:23:40.450
Here, we can
actually see that we

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:44.173
have it set up as a
target of 5-- that's

00:23:44.173 --> 00:23:45.340
what we configured in there.

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:48.250
The MINPODS is 1 and
the MAXPODS are 5.

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:51.950
And right now we don't
actually have any messages,

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:55.165
so the backlog is 0, and our
Deployment is actually fine.

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:04.170
Now, let's actually
generate some load.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:06.810
We'll do that by publishing
about 100 messages

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:07.905
to the topic itself.

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:15.310
All right.

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:17.890
Now that our messages are there,
let's take a look at the HPA

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:18.390
itself.

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:29.300
As you can see here, it's
actually updated now.

00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:31.970
We actually see that the value
that it's seeing here is 22,

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:34.100
and that it's looking
for a value of 5.

00:24:34.100 --> 00:24:47.600
So if we actually
describe the HPA,

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:51.230
we'll see that it actually wants
to trigger up and autoscale

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:54.140
our stuff up to the
max size of actual 5.

00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:55.715
Let's take a quick
look at our pods.

00:25:01.780 --> 00:25:04.570
You'll see that we've now
actually scaled up to 4 pods,

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:06.400
and we have a fifth pod pending.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:11.800
So as you can see here, we were
able to build an application,

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:16.150
have it subscribe,
set the metric based

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:19.600
on the unacknowledged backlog
from Google Cloud metrics,

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:23.750
and autoscale our application.

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:25.450
And once again, the
great thing here

00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:27.100
was we didn't have
to worry about any

00:25:27.100 --> 00:25:30.250
of the underlying compute
resources required for this

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:31.150
to actually work.

00:25:33.850 --> 00:25:34.350
All right.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:37.340
You'll see that our container
is actually creating.

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:40.590
And yeah, that's about
it for this demo.

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:41.480
So thanks, William.

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:43.127
